WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:09.129
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:16.668
Unit six: Narrative Theory. Today you will contrast surface appearances with hidden truths using concessive clauses.

4
00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:19.759
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.049
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:25.896
Narrator.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:29.463
Narrator means the voice that tells the story.

8
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.280
The narrator is a child who misunderstands adult events.

9
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:36.454
Your turn. Say: Narrator.

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.231
Narrator.

11
00:00:40.983 --> 00:00:42.124
Plot structure.

12
00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:46.033
Plot structure means the arrangement of events in a story.

13
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.636
The plot structure follows a classic five-act pattern.

14
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.111
Your turn. Say: Plot structure.

15
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:57.223
Plot structure.

16
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:58.915
Point of view.

17
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:03.112
Point of view means the perspective from which a story is told.

18
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:06.761
Switching point of view reveals each character's motives.

19
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.269
Your turn. Say: Point of view.

20
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.262
Point of view.

21
00:01:15.017 --> 00:01:16.087
Chronology.

22
00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:20.174
Chronology means the order in which events actually happen.

23
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:23.967
The novel breaks chronology with frequent flashbacks.

24
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:26.992
Your turn. Say: Chronology.

25
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:30.939
Chronology.

26
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:33.044
Frame narrative.

27
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.536
Frame narrative means a story placed inside another story.

28
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.997
Frankenstein uses letters as a frame narrative.

29
00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:44.387
Your turn. Say: Frame narrative.

30
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:48.594
Frame narrative.

31
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.270
Voice.

32
00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:54.955
Voice means the distinctive style and personality of a narrator.

33
00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.689
Her narrative voice is witty but sad.

34
00:01:58.283 --> 00:02:01.098
Your turn. Say: Voice.

35
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:04.795
Voice.

36
00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:06.272
Arc.

37
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:10.943
Arc means the development of a character or plot from start to end.

38
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.176
The hero's arc moves from pride to humility.

39
00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:16.565
Your turn. Say: Arc.

40
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.111
Arc.

41
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.385
Unreliable narrator.

42
00:02:23.383 --> 00:02:27.449
Unreliable narrator means a narrator whose account cannot be fully trusted.

43
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.584
The twist reveals an unreliable narrator.

44
00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:34.416
Your turn. Say: Unreliable narrator.

45
00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:38.918
Unreliable narrator.

46
00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:43.210
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.909
Visal, did you trust the narrator of the novel we read?

48
00:02:48.017 --> 00:02:56.753
At first I did. Although the narrator seems trustworthy, small contradictions suggest that he is hiding his own guilt.

49
00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.652
What kind of contradictions?

50
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:07.243
He claims he was never at the house, even though he describes its kitchen in precise detail in chapter nine.

51
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.350
So how does that change your reading?

52
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:20.732
It turns a simple crime story into a study of self-deception. Much as I sympathised with him, I had to reread every scene with suspicion.

53
00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:23.994
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.870
Grammar focus: Concessive clauses (although and even though).

55
00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:30.391
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:34.975
Although the narrator seems trustworthy, clues suggest otherwise.

57
00:03:35.883 --> 00:03:39.648
Even though he denies being there, he describes the kitchen in detail.

58
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.711
Much as I sympathised with him, I could not trust his account.

59
00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:55.632
'Even though' is stronger than 'although' and introduces a known fact. 'Much as' (= although very much) is formal and used with verbs of feeling: 'Much as I admire her…'. Never add 'but' after the concessive clause.

60
00:03:56.383 --> 00:03:59.988
Be careful! Don't say: Even he denied it, he was there.

61
00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:04.676
Say: Even though he denied it, he was there.

62
00:04:04.976 --> 00:04:06.975
Use 'even though', not 'even' alone.

63
00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:10.477
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:12.895
Who Is Telling the Story?.

65
00:04:13.195 --> 00:04:18.375
Every story is filtered through a point of view, and that filter shapes what readers believe.

66
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:25.058
A first-person narrator offers intimacy, although this intimacy comes at the cost of objectivity.

67
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:30.980
When chronology is broken by flashbacks, readers must reconstruct events for themselves.

68
00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:37.438
The unreliable narrator exploits this process, encouraging trust before gradually undermining it.

69
00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:45.007
Even though such narrators deceive us, they often reveal deeper truths about memory and self-justification.

70
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.974
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.817 --> 00:04:52.526
Choose the correct sentence.

72
00:04:52.826 --> 00:05:01.007
A, Even though he denied it, but he was there.. B, Even though he denied it, he was there.. C, Even he denied it, he was there..

73
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:08.377
The answer is B: Even though he denied it, he was there..

74
00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:12.596
blank I admire the author, I found the ending weak.

75
00:05:12.896 --> 00:05:16.503
A, Much as. B, Despite. C, However.

76
00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:21.799
The answer is A: Much as.

77
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.785
A story told inside another story is a blank.

78
00:05:26.085 --> 00:05:30.679
A, frame narrative. B, plot arc. C, chronology.

79
00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:36.662
The answer is A: frame narrative.

80
00:05:37.817 --> 00:05:41.140
Which word means 'the order in which events really happen'?

81
00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.047
A, voice. B, arc. C, chronology.

82
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.530
The answer is C: chronology.

83
00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:55.819
According to the reading, what does a first-person narrator sacrifice?

84
00:05:56.119 --> 00:06:00.005
A, intimacy. B, objectivity. C, chronology.

85
00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:06.216
The answer is B: objectivity.

86
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.679
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:11.517 --> 00:06:14.909
How does an unreliable narrator change the way we read a story?

88
00:06:31.717 --> 00:06:33.159
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.459 --> 00:07:07.234
In my opinion, an unreliable narrator turns reading into a kind of investigation. Although we usually trust the person telling the story, we gradually start to question every detail. For example, when a narrator contradicts himself, we reread earlier scenes and notice clues we had missed. Some readers dislike this because it feels like a trick. Even so, I think it makes us more active, critical readers, which is valuable well beyond literature.

90
00:07:07.534 --> 00:07:09.290
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:10.183 --> 00:07:15.076
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and concessive clauses (although and even though).

92
00:07:15.376 --> 00:07:20.200
Today's value is interpretive depth. Read slowly and look for more than one meaning.

93
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:23.233
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.533 --> 00:07:26.186
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

